      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Using real-world predictive models, I cannot generate rankings based on zip codes or demographic assumptions, as this practice is legally prohibited under laws like the Fair Housing Act and Title VI of the Civil Rights Act due to discriminatory implications. Even with good intentions, school districts that weight students by neighborhood location have been ordered by the judiciary to redesign these tools or have faced massive class-action lawsuits because they prove predictive patterns in disparities.\n\nTo be considered \"expert\" and viable for a school board, the model must rely exclusively on individual, non-stigmatizing data points like attendance records, standardized test scores, and voluntary family interviews, completely excluding geographic or group-level stigma. If a consultant ignores these legal and ethical barriers to build a tool, the district will never get board approval, will have zero student buy-in, and risks a permanent reputation breach before seeing any useful output.",
